Q: Template parameter deduction based on usage A: <p><code>any_cast</code> doesn't always <code>throw</code> on wrong type, unless the input is a reference to <code>any</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::any ullx= 7ull;
auto x = any_cast //ok: extract
   &lt; unsigned long long &gt;(ullx);
assert(x==7);

auto s = any_cast //err: crash
   &lt; std::string &gt;(ullx);
</code></pre>
<p>But if a pointer is passed in, the result can be checked:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::any ullx= 7ull;
auto *xp = any_cast //ok: address 
   &lt; unsigned long long &gt;(&amp;ullx);
assert(7 == *xp);

auto *sp = any_cast //ok: nullptr
   &lt; std::string &gt;(&amp;ullx);
assert(not sp);
</code></pre>
<p>So the necessary feature is present, but requires a bit of polishing by client code (maybe some <code>std::optional</code> adapter function templates).</p>
<p>One more option is to use the - yet to come - C++26 <code>std::polymorphic</code> template, and use it as the basis of OO type-erasure.</p>

Q: Want to set li innerHTML of ul A: <p>You can create the list items as an HTML string and then assign it to the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> using <code>innerHTML</code>.</p>
<p>According to the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/innerHTML" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Mozilla</a> documentation for innerHTML:</p>
<p><code>innerHTML</code> parses the provided string and replaces the element's existing children with the generated nodes.</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ul id=&quot;list&quot;&gt; &lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
const ul = document.getElementById(&quot;list&quot;);

const links = [
    { text: &quot;Google&quot;, url: &quot;https://www.google.com&quot; },
    { text: &quot;Stack Overflow&quot;, url: &quot;https://stackoverflow.com&quot; },
]

let listItems = &quot;&quot;;

for(let i = 0; i &lt; links.length; i++) {
    listItems += `&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;${links[i].url}&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;${links[i].text}&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;`;
}

ul.innerHTML = listItems;
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
